{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/lecturer-and-laboratory-coordinator-department-of-physics-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states-3018b01e-1a58-48be-97c3-9f2f32694eab": {
      "title": "Lecturer and Laboratory Coordinator - Department of Physics",
      "description": "The Department of Physics at Seton Hall University seeks applications for a full-time Lecturer position beginning in the Fall 2027 semester. The successful applicant will contribute to undergraduate and graduate instruction, specializing in applied physics and physics-based engineering curriculum. We welcome educators who integrate theoretical physics with practical applications and emerging technologies, demonstrating a strong commitment to teaching excellence and supporting students from diverse backgrounds.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach undergraduate physics courses across the curriculum, including introductory physics, applied physics, and upper-level courses for physics, science, and engineering-related programs.\n• Teach graduate-level courses in physics and applied physics, as assigned and consistent with the candidate’s academic preparation and disciplinary expertise.\n• Develop and deliver clear, rigorous, and engaging course content that integrates physics fundamentals with engineering applications, quantitative analysis, laboratory or computational methods, design-oriented problem-solving, and relevant technologies.\n• Assess student learning, provide timely feedback, and maintain appropriate academic standards.\n• Advise and mentor students in support of their academic progress and professional development.\n• Collaborate with Department faculty and related academic programs to support curriculum development, course coordination, and continuous improvement in physics, applied physics, and engineering-oriented instruction.\n• Participate in departmental, college, and university service as appropriate to the Lecturer role.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Doctorate in Physics, Applied Physics, Physics Education, or a closely related field by the date of appointment. Candidates with a Ph.D., Ed.D., or other relevant terminal doctorate are encouraged to apply. Candidates whose highest relevant terminal degree is a Master’s degree will also be considered if they have prior college- or university-level teaching experience in physics, applied physics, engineering physics, or a closely related field; preference will be given to candidates with doctoral degrees.\n• Demonstrated ability or strong potential to teach physics, applied physics, and physics-based engineering courses effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels.\n• Commitment to inclusive teaching and to supporting students with varied educational and cultural backgrounds.\n• Effective written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills.\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Prior college- or university-level teaching experience in physics, applied physics, engineering physics, or closely related courses.\n• Evidence of teaching effectiveness and familiarity with student-centered or evidence-based instructional methods.\n• Ability to teach a broad range of physics courses and contribute to applied physics and engineering curricula in areas such as electronics, instrumentation, materials, energy, computation, or other technologically relevant fields.\nLicenses and Certificates:\nN/A\nSalary Range:\n$49,669-$55,188\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nApplicants should submit a curriculum vitae; a teaching philosophy and approaches statement; a statement describing their approach to inclusive excellence in teaching, mentoring, and academic community-building; and the names and full contact information of three professional references. Review of applications will begin in late Fall 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to the Chair of Search Committee, Prof. Jose L. Lopez, e-mail: jose.lopez1@shu.edu\nBenefits Information:\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/tenure-track-assistant-professor-of-applied-physics-engineering-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Applied Physics & Engineering",
      "description": "The Department of Physics at Seton Hall University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Applied Physics & Engineering, starting in Fall 2027. We seek an outstanding scholar advancing computational science and theoretical methods in physics, applied physics, or engineering. Priority will be given to candidates specializing in plasma physics and engineering, semiconductor physics, or materials science. Key areas of interest include computational modeling and simulation, theory-driven research, high-performance computing, and machine learning/AI applications in STEM. Interdisciplinary researchers who bridge these domains are strongly encouraged to apply. While computation and theory are preferred, exceptional experimentalists whose research integrates computational or theoretical components within our priority areas will also be considered. The successful candidate will build a prominent, externally funded research program, leverage high-performance computing resources, and actively mentor undergraduate and graduate student researchers.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research program in applied physics or engineering, with priority given to computational and theoretical work.\n• Use high-performance computing and modern computational methods to address significant scientific or engineering questions.\n• Integrate machine learning and artificial intelligence, where appropriate, into research, modeling, simulation, data analysis, or scientific discovery.\n• Teach 18 credits per academic year in undergraduate and graduate physics, applied physics, and engineering courses, including fundamental lecture or laboratory courses as needed.\n• Develop new courses that strengthen the Department’s offerings in computational physics, applied physics, and engineering.\n• Mentor undergraduate and graduate students in research and professional development.\n• Advise physics students and contribute service to the Department, University, profession, and broader community.\n• Build interdisciplinary collaborations within STEM and with external academic, government, or industry partners.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Ph.D. in Physics, Applied Physics, Engineering, Materials Science, or a closely related field by the time of appointment.\n• Evidence of the potential to establish a productive, independent research program.\n• Demonstrated preparation to teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels.\n• Commitment to mentoring and supporting students from a wide range of backgrounds.\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Expertise in computational science or theoretical physics and engineering, especially in plasma physics and engineering, semiconductor physics, or materials science.\n• Experience developing and using numerical models, large-scale simulations, scientific software, or data-intensive computational workflows.\n• Experience with high-performance computing environments, including parallel or distributed computing.\n• Experience applying machine learning or artificial intelligence to scientific or engineering research.\n• A record of peer-reviewed scholarship and strong potential to secure external research support.\n• Potential to complement existing departmental strengths and contribute to interdisciplinary research and curricular development.\n• For experimental candidates, a research program that connects productively with computation, theory, advanced data analysis, or the Department’s priority areas.\nLicenses and Certificates:\nN/A\nSalary Range:\n$64,500-$71,600\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nApplicants should submit a curriculum vitae; a research statement describing current work, future plans, anticipated computing needs, and opportunities for student involvement; a teaching statement; a statement describing their approach to inclusive excellence in teaching, mentoring, and academic community-building; and the names and full contact information of three professional references. Review of applications will begin in late Fall 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to the Chair of Search Committee, Prof. Jose L. Lopez, e-mail: jose.lopez1@shu.edu\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/lecturer-information-technology-management-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Information Technology Management",
      "description": "The Department of Computing and Decision Sciences invites applications for a full-time faculty position in Information Technology Management beginning August 30, 2027, available as a renewable term appointment. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in IT management, enterprise systems, data analytics, and emerging technologies; develop curriculum; advise students; and contribute to departmental service and program initiatives. Candidates should hold a Ph.D. (or be ABD) in in Information Systems, IT Management, Business Analytics, or a related field, or a master’s degree with significant industry and teaching experience. Desired qualifications include expertise in AI, data-driven decision-making, and technology innovation, along with strong communication skills and a commitment to diversity and inclusion. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and selected candidates may meet faculty during the Decision Sciences Institute Meeting in San Francisco.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in areas such as information technology management, enterprise systems, data analytics, and emerging technologies.\n• Develop new courses and update existing curriculum to reflect current trends in information systemsand technology innovation.\n• Advise students and participate in departmental and university service activities.\n• Contribute to departmental initiatives in program development, assessment, and interdisciplinary collaboration.\n• Integrate applied, real-world experiences and technology trends into classroom instruction.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Ph.D. or Master's degree in Information Systems, Information Technology Management, Business Analytics, or a closely related field.\n• Significant industry experience applying technology and analytics to business challenges (e.g.,operations, marketing, finance, or supply chain).\n• Prior teaching experience as a full-time or adjunct instructor.\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Knowledge or experience with artificial intelligence (AI), data-driven decision-making, and technology innovation.\n• Ability to teach both quantitative and managerial aspects of information systems.\n• Industry experience that enables integration of practical applications into academic settings.\n• Evidence of strong communication and interpersonal skills.\n• Commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion in teaching, research, and service.\nLicenses and Certificates:\n• No specific licenses or certifications are required.\n• Professional certifications in IT management, analytics, or project management (e.g., PMP, CBIP, CISSP, AWS, or similar) are considered advantageous.\nSalary Range:\n$91,800- $102,000\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nContact information of three references\nBenefits Information:\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-business-analytics-and-supply-chain-management-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Business Analytics and Supply Chain Management",
      "description": "The position seeks a faculty member to teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in Business Analytics and Supply Chain Management, covering areas such as data analytics, supply chain analytics, procurement, operations management, and data-driven decision-making. Responsibilities include conducting and publishing scholarly research, engaging with industry to enhance teaching and partnerships, advising students, and contributing to departmental and university service. Candidates should hold a Ph.D. (or be ABD) in a relevant field for tenure-track consideration. Desired qualifications include practical business analytics and supply chain management experience, familiarity with AI applications, curriculum development skills, and a strong research agenda. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and selected candidates may meet faculty during the Decision Sciences Institute Meeting in San Fransisco. The university is an equal opportunity employer committed to diversity.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nThe successful candidate for this position will be responsible for the following:\n• Teaching and Curriculum Development: Design, teach, and continually update undergraduate and graduate-level courses in Business Analytics and Supply Chain Management, and related fields. Specific areas of instruction include data analytics, supply chain management, procurement, operations management, and data-driven decision-making.\n• Research and Scholarship: Engage in scholarly research with a focus on advancing the field of business analytics and supply chain management. Publish research in peer-reviewed journals.\n• Industry Engagement: Apply practical experience to enrich the classroom with real-world case studies, guest speakers, and industry connections. Foster relationships between the department and industry partners.\n• Service to the Department and University: Participate in departmental and university committees, collaborate with faculty on initiatives, and contribute to the development of academic programs.\n• Advising and Mentorship: Provide academic advising to undergraduate and graduate students and support their career development in the business analytics and supply chain management fields.\nRequired Qualifications:\no Ph.D. in Business Analytics, Supply Chain Management, Quantitative Methods, or a closely related field.\no ABD candidates will be considered; however, the doctorate must be completed by the appointment date of August 1, 2027.\no Evidence of teaching effectiveness at the university level, either through previous full-time or adjunct instructor roles.\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Experience with or knowledge of artificial intelligence (AI) applications in analytics or supply chain.\n• Practical industry experience in supply chain management, with specific expertise in areas such as logistics, procurement, inventory management, or operations.\n• Strong ability to teach quantitative aspects of supply chain management.\n• Demonstrated ability to integrate real-world case studies and foster industry relationships in the classroom.\n• Evidence of a strong research agenda and interest in publishing in peer-reviewed journals.\n• Previous experience in curriculum development and course design, especially for both undergraduate and graduate programs.\nLicenses and Certificates:\n• No specific licenses or certificates are required for this position; however, professional certifications such as Certified Supply Chain Professional (CSCP) or other relevant industry certifications would be considered a plus.\nSalary Range:\n$139,500- $155,000\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nContact information of three references\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/lecturer-department-of-mathematics-and-computer-science-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Lecturer- Department of Mathematics and Computer Science",
      "description": "The Department of Mathematics and Computer Science at Seton Hall University invites applications for a Full-Time Lecturer position in Mathematics to start in August 2027.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its faculty respects Catholic beliefs and values, engages the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and supports the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• The Department offers undergraduate majors in Computer Science, Mathematics, and Financial Mathematics; minors in Computer Science and Mathematics, and an undergraduate minor and a graduate certificate program (with the Department of Psychology) in Data Visualization and Analysis.\n• Teaching responsibilities will include mathematics courses at all undergraduate levels.\n• Duties also involve performing service to the Department.\n• Applicants must understand and be willing to support the Seton Hall University Catholic mission.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• A minimum of a Ph.D. degree in Mathematics or a related field is a requirement and must be completed by the start of employment (no later than August 2027).\n• Proficiency in using mathematical software and technology is highly desired.\nDesired Qualifications:\n• A minimum of three years teaching experience at the college level.\n• Experience in teaching Calculus-based Statistics is highly desired.\nSalary Range:\n$58,500- $65,000\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPosition is contingent on budgetary approval.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-tenure-track-sustainable-biology-and-environmental-science-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states-e0357c25-83d0-438d-a8a1-8caa050e3611": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor- Tenure Track - Sustainable Biology and Environmental Science",
      "description": "Seton Hall University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track professor at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Department of Biological Sciences to start Fall 2027, pending final budgetary approval.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences is undergoing rapid growth and seeks to develop coursework and research in environmental sciences and related fields.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences offers a diverse research environment including PhD/MS graduate programs, core facilities, and a collaborative environment. Additional information on the department and its research faculty is described here: https://www.shu.edu/biology/index.cfm\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its faculty respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nThe successful candidate will develop a research program that involves both undergraduate and graduate students and can be externally funded. The position includes teaching biology major required courses, ecology focused courses, as well as graduate courses within their area of expertise. Faculty are also expected to mentor Graduate students’ research; offer experiential research opportunities with advising to undergraduate students; and provide services to the department, college, and university.\nMore specifically, the hire would be expected to (1) run a core biology courses & support the biology majors in all sciences; (2) increase the variety of upper-level bio-electives by offering new courses such as Sustainable Agriculture and Habitat Restoration or Environmental Toxicology and participate in the ongoing creation of academic programs in Environmental Science; (3) assist with educating, managing, mentoring, and advising the increased number of biology majors; (4) provide a computational-based research space for undergrads and graduate students; (5) assist with all the day today activities that are important to a functional department; and (6), just as importantly, the faculty member will participate in innovative, collaborative program development in interdisciplinary STEM fields as part of our “STEM Initiative.” This concentration will provide student support for occupations in “Green Fields” including environmental engineering, conservation scientists, and urban planning.\nRequired Qualifications:\nApplicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Environmental Sciences or related fields (Ecology, Marine Biology, Environmental Science, Plant Biology, Botany, Geology, Earth Science). Preferred candidates would have 2yrs of relevant postdoctoral training, a strong publication record, and previous teaching experience at the college level. (ABDs will be considered)\nDesired Qualifications:\nApplicants will be expected to establish and sustain an energetic, externally funded research program. The successful candidate will mentor graduate student research/projects and offer experiential research opportunities to undergraduate students.\nThe candidate will teach graduate and undergraduate students. Courses will include general biology major courses, upper-level biology major electives, as well as graduate courses in his/her area of expertise.\nThe University is interested in expanding its programming in environmental sciences and engineering, and the ideal candidate would be interested in contributing to that effort.\nThey will engage in service for the department, college, and university, including, but not limited to, committee work and undergraduate student advising.\nTo apply for this position, upload:\nCover letter including job code number\nCurriculum vitae\n3-page research plan\nTeaching philosophy\n3 current letters of references\nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received before December 1, 2026 will receive priority review\nSeton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer with a commitment to increasing diversity among our faculty. We encourage applications from women, minorities, and other under-represented groups.\nSalary Range:\n$68,483 -$76,093\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-tenure-track-immunology-and-microbiology-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor- Tenure Track - Immunology and Microbiology",
      "description": "Seton Hall University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track professor at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Department of Biological Sciences with a specialization in Microbiology and Immunology to start Fall 2027, pending final budgetary approval.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences is undergoing rapid growth and seeks to develop coursework and research in microbiology and immunology and related fields. Lab startup funds are available.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences offers a diverse research environment including PhD/MS graduate programs, core facilities, and a collaborative environment. Additional information on the department and its research faculty is described here: https://www.shu.edu/biology/index.cfm\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its faculty respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeaching\nTeach 18 credits per academic year at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, focusing on core biology courses and supporting the academic progress of biology majors across diverse scientific disciplines. Expand the range of upper-level bio-electives by introducing or teaching specialized courses such as Pathophysiology, Immunotherapeutic Design, and Metabolic Diseases, catering to evolving student interests and the demands of the Health-STEM workforce. Contribute courses to the environmental minor, such as Microbial Epidemiology, and maintain small class sizes to ensure meaningful student-faculty interactions and enhance learning outcomes.\nResearch and Scholarship\nLead high-impact biomedical research in immunology and microbiology research areas that overlap with other core science fields such as genetics, physiology, bioinformatics, cancer, and cell biology, aimed at advancing scientific understanding of diseases, developing novel treatments, and improving patient outcomes. Establish a state-of-the-art Biomedical Science research laboratory offering dedicated space for undergraduate and graduate students to engage in research projects, providing hands-on experience that fosters their intellectual growth and prepares them for their careers. Foster interdisciplinary collaborations within and beyond the department, cultivating a dynamic research environment that promotes innovation, scholarly exploration, and research excellence. Support the development and launch of a new BS in Microbiology & Immunology major, aligning research efforts with educational goals and preparing students for careers in the rapidly growing field of microbiology.\nStudent Mentoring and Advising\nMentor, manage, and advise a growing number of medical-focused biology majors, providing personalized guidance to support their academic and professional development. Mentor graduate students' research and offer experiential research opportunities with advising to undergraduate students engaged in the laboratory.\nService Commitments\nActively participate in departmental, college, and university service activities, contributing to the academic and administrative functions of the institution. Collaborate with colleagues to address departmental needs, support institutional initiatives, and promote a collegial, collaborative work environment\nRequired Qualifications:\nApplicants must have a Ph.D. in Biological Sciences, Biomedical Sciences, Immunology, Microbiology or a closely related field, with a research focus in one or more of the following areas: immunology, microbiology, genetics, bioinformatics, or cell biology. Two years of postdoctoral research experience in immunology, microbiology, genetics, or a related biomedical discipline is preferred. A demonstrated record of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed publications commensurate with career stage. Have evidence of, or clear potential for, externally funded research and previous teaching experience at the college level.\nDesired Qualifications:\nExperience teaching or developing upper-level courses relevant to health-STEM fields. Prior success securing extramural funding as a PI or co-PI. Experience integrating computational or bioinformatics approaches into biomedical research.\nTo apply for this position, upload:\n1) Cover letter including job code number\n2) Curriculum vitae\n3) 3-page research plan\n4) Teaching philosophy\n5) 3 current letters of reference\nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received before December 1, 2026 will receive priority review.\nSeton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer with a commitment to increasing diversity among our faculty. We encourage applications from women, minorities, and other under-represented groups.\nSalary Range:\n$68,483 -$76,093\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/term-faculty-english-department-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Term Faculty-English Department",
      "description": "The English Department at Seton Hall University invites applications for a Contract Faculty Member to join our First-Year Writing Program. We specifically welcome scholars specializing in rhetoric and composition, writing studies, or related fields. Pending budgetary approval from the board, the appointment will begin with the Fall 2027 semester (Aug. 15, 2027) and can be renewed yearly for up to five years. Applicants should submit a cover letter, teaching philosophy, and current vita by January 1, 2027. Preliminary interviews will take place virtually. Invited candidates will then be asked to submit three letters of recommendation. Seton Hall University is a Catholic diocesan university and an EO/AA employer.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nThe standard teaching load for contract faculty is 12 credits per semester. Course assignments will be balanced between our two first-year writing courses, a composition and rhetoric course and a composition and literature course. There may be opportunities to teach other courses to support department needs.\nRequired Qualifications:\nMaster’s Degree in Writing or related fields required; PhD Preferred.\nSalary Range:\n$55,080- $61,200\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states-95dc538d-d2b8-4dad-822a-efb7e65f419d": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "The Department of Mathematics and Computer Science at Seton Hall University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Data Science, beginning in August 2027. The position will be housed in the Mathematics & Computer Science department at Seton Hall University.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nPriority will be given to applicants with research in areas of Data Science, broadly construed. Responsibilities will include teaching computer science courses at all undergraduate levels and, depending on the candidate's expertise, undergraduate and graduate courses in Data Science and Engineering, including service courses. Additional responsibilities include supervising student research, advising, recruitment, and active publication and grantsmanship in the field.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA Ph.D. in Data Science, Applied Mathematics, or Computer Science, or a closely related field is required and must be completed by the start date of employment. The candidate’s Ph.D. must be completed by the start of employment.\nDesired Qualifications:\nWe welcome applicants from all areas of data science, with particular interest in Machine Learning. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in teaching, scholarly research, and the mentorship of undergraduate and graduate students through research experiences.\nSalary Range:\n$98,500- $109,500\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nAll applicants are required to provide a teaching statement, research statement and diversity statement (explaining what diversity means to the applicant with respect to Computer Science and to a university community). Please also include a statement waiving the right to see reference letters.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/criminal-justice-tenure-track-assistant-professor-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Criminal Justice Tenure-Track Assistant Professor",
      "description": "The Department of Sociology, Anthropology, and Criminal Justice in the College of Arts and Sciences at Seton Hall University invites applications for an Assistant Professor position, with an anticipated start date of August 15, 2027. The Criminal Justice program consists of a 36-credit hour curriculum and serves nearly 300 undergraduate majors and minors. The ideal candidate should be able to teach a broad range of undergraduate courses in Criminal Justice and Criminology including, but not limited to, criminology theory, qualitative methods, and senior seminar, and hold a Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or Sociology.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nThe successful candidate will be expected to engage in an active research program and teach Criminal Justice and Criminology courses at the undergraduate level. The candidate is also expected to be proficient in academic advising and student mentoring, program and curricular development, and other institutional service activities.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or Sociology from an accredited university, with a dissertation related to Criminal Justice or Criminology. ABD candidates may be considered. A Juris Doctorate (J.D.) will not be considered sufficient.\n• A demonstrated record of and agenda for continued scholarship in Criminal Justice or Criminology.\n• Undergraduate teaching experience in one or more of the following areas: courts, criminal law, or corrections.\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Research or teaching interests in one of the following areas: courts, corrections, homeland security, big data, machine learning/artificial intelligence.\n• Leadership and mentorship in teaching, research, or service that supports an equitable and diverse scholarly environment.\n• Experience in student advisement.\n• Prior professional experience relevant to assigned course topics.\n• Able to develop online and hybrid modes of course delivery.\nLicenses and Certificates:\nExempt\nSalary Range:\n$70,108- $77,898\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\n• Screening of applications begins on December 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled.\n• The preferred position start date is August 15, 2027.\n• A complete application consists of: (1) a letter of application addressing the qualifications cited above, including past and/or potential contributions to diversity, equity, and inclusion; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) teaching statement; (4) research statement; and (5) the names and contact information of at least three professional references. Other documentation may be requested once a candidate is under consideration.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/scholarly-communications-librarian-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Scholarly Communications Librarian",
      "description": "Seton Hall University Libraries invites applications for our Scholarly Communications Librarian position. This is a permanent full-time, non-tenure track Lecturer position. We seek a tech-savvy individual to work with faculty and systems to showcase, manage, and analyze SHU’s scholarly output.\nReporting to the Assistant Dean for Information Technology and Collection Services, the Scholarly Communications Librarian performs a variety of work with library faculty and staff, faculty departments, and campus partners to develop and implement research information management software, assist with the institutional repository and faculty metrics. The successful candidate will develop promotional material and provide training and support online and in-person for the SHU community.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n- Facilitates requests for faculty scholarly metrics reports; provides consultation services to faculty for creating a faculty profile page and on the use of research metrics, from various bibliometric and altmetric resources, such as Scopus, SHU’s institutional repository (Digital Commons), ORCID, Faculty Success, Pure4EGS, and other similar platforms.\n- Primary point of contact for the Libraries’ role in finalizing and registering doctoral dissertations and master’s theses..\n- Creates and maintains training and marketing material related to research information management.\n- Prepares reports and presentations for faculty, departments, and research centers.\n- Works closely with external vendors and campus partners to solve technical issues related to scholarly communications platforms.\n- Participates in reference, library instruction, and leading workshops.\n- Participates as an active member of the Libraries’ Research Data Services unit.\n- Assists in assessment and migration of new technologies.\n- Assists in the implementation of new digital services.\n- Creates and enhances resources for scholarly communications related topics, including compliance with IRB guidelines.\nRequired Qualifications:\n- ALA-accredited master's degree in library science and information studies.\n- Minimum of 1-3 years relevant experience.\n- Experience with institutional repository software such as Digital Commons.\n- Familiarity with scholarly metrics and digital identifiers such as ORCID and data sources (e.g. Scopus, Google Scholar, and SciVal).\n- Basic knowledge of copyright and Open Access principles a.\n- Experience with project management, solving technical issues, and communicating with vendors.\n- Experience with consulting, training, and writing technical documentation.\n- Excellent communication skills (listening, speaking, and writing).\n- Experience with reference and teaching.\n-Commitment to advancing diversity and inclusion in our workplace, as well as in the systems, services, and collections we build\n- Commitment to supporting the Mission of the University and servant leadership\nDesired Qualifications:\n- Experience overseeing staff and student assistants.\n- Experience with research information management tools.\n- Experience with data visualization and data analysis.\n- Current knowledge of web and digital content technologies, and standards of interoperability, usability, and accessibility for the web.\n- Experience with ATLAS.ti, Qualtrics, ArcGIS, STATA, and/or MATLAB.\n- Experience teaching information literacy classes.\n- Experience teaching data services workshops.\nSalary Range:\n$58,359- $64,843\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease provide 3 professional references that we may contact.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-of-classics-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Classics",
      "description": "The Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures at Seton Hall University invites applications for a tenure-track position in Classics at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning in the Fall of 2027. We seek a dynamic scholar and dedicated teacher with a strong record of scholarly achievement and demonstrated excellence in undergraduate education. The successful candidate will have broad expertise in the field of Classics and be prepared to teach both Greek and Latin at a range of levels, from introductory through advanced courses, as well as elective courses of interest to a general student population and courses within the University Core and the Honors Program. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research and teaching engage creatively with the classical world and who can contribute to the intellectual vitality and curricular development of our undergraduate program. Candidates should demonstrate an active and promising program of research, as evidenced by publications, scholarly presentations, and/or other significant contributions to the field. We welcome applicants working in any area of Classics, including but not limited to Greek and Roman literature, history, culture, archaeology, religion, philosophy, reception studies, or the classical tradition.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nThe successful candidate will also demonstrate a strong record of teaching excellence and a commitment to innovative, student-centered pedagogy. In addition to teaching courses in Greek and Latin language and literature, the candidate will be expected to develop innovative and engaging undergraduate courses that expand the study of the ancient Mediterranean world and connect Classics with broader intellectual, cultural, and contemporary concerns. We especially welcome candidates who can contribute to interdisciplinary teaching and who are interested in developing courses that attract students from across the university. Finally, as the director of the Classics Program, the successful candidate will be expected to engage in creative program building activities that engage students and faculty across campus.\nRequired Qualifications:\nApplicants must hold a Ph.D. in Classics or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Candidates should demonstrate:\n•A strong record of, or clear potential for, scholarly excellence and publication;\n•Demonstrated excellence in undergraduate teaching;\n•Advanced proficiency in both Ancient Greek and Latin;\n•The ability to teach Greek and Latin language and literature at multiple levels;\n•A commitment to innovative and inclusive approaches to undergraduate education;\n•The ability to develop new courses that engage students with Classics from interdisciplinary and/or contemporary perspectives.\nSalary Range:\n$59,091-$65,657\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nApplicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, research statement, evidence of teaching effectiveness, and three letters of recommendation. Due date is November 20, 2026.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/associate-professor-school-of-law-newark-new-jersey-united-states-588b5d0d-be03-4b5f-8bf1-07aee3223c93": {
      "title": "Associate Professor, School of Law",
      "description": "Seton Hall University School of Law welcomes applications for a tenure-track position to begin July 1, 2027. Candidates should demonstrate (i) scholarly promise, (ii) the ability or potential to be an outstanding teacher and mentor who can prepare students for the practice of law, and (iii) leadership in service toward building an equitable and diverse academic community.\nWe will consider entry-level and lateral candidates. We have particular teaching needs in tax law.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nSeton Hall Law School offers a vibrant, energetic academic environment. Located in downtown Newark, New Jersey, approximately 20 minutes from Manhattan, Seton Hall Law is especially well-regarded in the health and life sciences law, intellectual property, cybersecurity, and privacy arenas, and it is in the process of expanding its role in energy, gaming/sports, technology and data analytics.\nThe main campus of Seton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, about 4 miles from the Law School . Seton Hall University is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and support the university’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nRequired Qualifications:\nCandidates must have a J.D. or equivalent degree and a record of academic excellence.\nSalary Range:\n$153,855-$170,951\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nThe following documents should be uploaded with your application: CV (with references), cover letter, scholarly agenda, and job talk paper (or abstract with expected date for completion of the draft).\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-professor-environmental-studies-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor-Environmental Studies",
      "description": "The Environmental Studies Program at Seton Hall University (https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-environmental-studies.html) invites applications for an adjunct instructor of Environmental Studies for Spring 2027. The ideal candidate has an interdisciplinary background in the environmental social sciences or sustainability sciences, with both research and teaching experience. Applicants must be prepared to teach Introduction to Environmental Studies, our signature introductory survey course for both majors and non-majors.\nSeton Hall University is in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states with alumni from more than 60 countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nEnvironmental Studies at Seton Hall is an interdisciplinary program in the College of Arts & Sciences that draws on faculty expertise in the natural sciences, social sciences, and humanities to study the complex relationships between humans and their environment. Students also learn by doing, with credit-bearing internships and other experiential learning opportunities.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nResponsibilities include teaching one 1000-level course (Introduction to Environmental Studies) during the Spring 2027 semester, with the possibility for renewal in future semesters.\nRequired Qualifications:\nPhD in Environmental Studies, Sustainability Education, Environmental Policy, Geography, Anthropology, Sociology, or related fields. ABD candidates may be considered but the degree must be completed at the time of appointment.\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1230 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1332.50 per credit for those with a terminal degree.\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nTo Apply: Please submit a brief cover letter describing your relevant experience and teaching interests, along with a current CV and contact information for three references who can speak to your qualifications. Applicants are welcome, but not required, to include a sample syllabus, recent teaching evaluations, or other evidence of teaching effectiveness. Applications should be submitted prior to November 1, 2026, at which point review of applications will begin and will continue until the position is filled. For questions about the position, please contact Director of Environmental Studies, Dr. Jacob Weger.\nBenefits Information:\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-of-computer-science-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science",
      "description": "The Department of Mathematics and Computer Science at Seton Hall University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Computer Science. The appointment will begin in August 2027.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nPreference will be given to candidates whose research expertise aligns with one or more of the following areas: cloud and edge computing, cyber-physical systems, and AI systems.\nResponsibilities include teaching computer science courses at all undergraduate levels and, depending on the candidate's expertise, undergraduate and graduate courses in Cybersecurity and/or Data Science, including service courses. Additional responsibilities include supervising student research and maintaining a computer engineering laboratory.\nApplicants should understand and be willing to support Seton Hall University's Catholic mission.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field is required and must be completed by the start date of employment. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in teaching, scholarly research, and the mentorship of students through undergraduate and graduate research experiences.\nDesired Qualifications:\nTeaching experience and a record of scholarly publications are preferred.\nSalary Range:\n$98,625- $109,583\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nProvide e-mail contact information for 3 references which may be contacted to provide recommendation letters.\nProvide a description of research goals\nProvide a diversity statement\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/practicum-director-master-of-social-work-program-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Practicum Director - Master of Social Work Program",
      "description": "The Practicum Director provides leadership and administration for the MSW practicum education program while supporting student recruitment, admissions, enrollment, retention, and community partnerships. The Director ensures practicum operations align with MSW Program objectives, University policies, and applicable CSWE accreditation standards.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nPracticum Administration\nOversee all aspects of practicum education, including placement processes, policies, documentation, timelines, student evaluations, and program operations.\nStudent Placement and Support\nCoordinate student placements from initial readiness assessment through placement confirmation and completion. Address placement challenges, student performance concerns, agency issues, and necessary remediation or placement changes.\nAgency and Community Partnerships\nRecruit, evaluate, and maintain a diverse network of high-quality practicum sites and qualified practicum instructors. Develop strategic partnerships with nonprofit, government, healthcare, educational, behavioral health, and community organizations.\nPracticum Instructor Support\nOrient and support practicum instructors regarding supervision expectations, student competencies, evaluations, University requirements, and professional standards.\nCompliance and Accreditation\nMaintain practicum records, affiliation agreements, assessment data, and processes necessary to support University requirements and CSWE accreditation. Participate in accreditation reporting, assessment, program review, and continuous improvement activities.\nStudent Recruitment\nParticipate actively in MSW recruitment through information sessions, open houses, recruitment events, community outreach, and prospective student engagement. Develop recruitment pipelines through universities, employers, alumni, community organizations, and practicum partners.\nAdmissions and Application Review\nParticipate in MSW admissions by reviewing applications, evaluating applicant readiness, conducting interviews when appropriate, and making admissions recommendations. Support Advanced Standing review, admitted-student outreach, and enrollment yield initiatives.\nStudent Success and Retention\nCollaborate with program leadership and faculty to identify barriers to student progression and implement strategies supporting retention, professional development, and successful degree completion.\nProgram and Partnership Development\nRepresent the MSW Program with community organizations, professional associations, alumni, and prospective partners. Identify opportunities that strengthen practicum education, recruitment, employment pipelines, community engagement, and overall program growth.\nAdministration and Supervision\nSupervise graduate assistants and practicum-related administrative support as assigned and oversee practicum management systems, student and agency records, and operational workflows.\nRequired Qualifications:\nMSW from a CSWE-accredited program.\nMinimum two years of post-MSW social work practice experience.\nStrong administrative, organizational, communication, and relationship-building skills.\nExperience working with students, interns, supervisees, or emerging professionals.\nAbility to develop community partnerships and manage multiple priorities.\nRequired teaching of 1 course per semester.\nPosition Expectations\nThe Practicum Director serves as an educational administrator and strategic partner responsible for strengthening practicum education, student recruitment, admissions, student success, community partnerships, and MSW Program growth. Local and regional travel for site development, recruitment, and community engagement is expected.\nDesired Qualifications:\nExperience in social work practicum/field education or higher education.\nExperience with student recruitment, graduate admissions, or enrollment.\nExperience with CSWE accreditation and program assessment.\nExperience with Tevera or a comparable practicum management system.\nSocial work licensure preferred; advanced clinical licensure is not required solely by CSWE standards.\nPhD in Social Work, Public Administration, or a related field is preferred.\nLicenses and Certificates:\nLCSW\nSalary Range:\n$60,000-$72,000\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/contract-faculty-department-of-physics-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Contract Faculty - Department of Physics",
      "description": "The Department of Physics at Seton Hall University invites applications to fill a full-time contract teaching position beginning the Fall 2026 semester. A Ph.D. in Physics or related field is required. The candidate is expected to teach all levels of Physics courses. We seek a candidate who demonstrates excellence in teaching lectures and laboratory courses at all levels, especially to students of diverse backgrounds.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and respect its mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeaching lecture and laboratory physics courses at all undergraduate and graduate levels.\nA total teaching load 12 credits/semester\nRequired Qualifications:\nA Ph.D. in Physics or related field is required.\nDesired Qualifications:\nOne or more years of teaching experience.\nSalary Range:\n$49,669-$55,188\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nBenefits Information:\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-foundations-fine-art-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Foundations - Fine Art",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media of Seton Hall University invites applications for part-time adjunct instructors to teach on-campus in our Art and Design program. The College seeks to engage individuals who value creativity, diversity, inclusion, and collaboration.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach up to two courses per semester\n• Each class meets once a week for 2.5 hours\n• For additional information about the program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-art-design.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Expertise and ability to teach in one or more of the following areas: drawing, 2D design and color, watercolor, figure drawing, illustration, digital photography\n• Earned MFA degree in visual art\nDesired Qualifications:\n• A minimum of one year teaching experience at the college level\nLicenses and Certificates:\n• Earned terminal degree in visual art or design\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral studio classroom environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nRequired Documents\n• Resume or Curriculum Vitae\n• Cover Letter\n• Three professional references with contact information: address, phone number, and email address\nOptional Documents\n• Samples of current artwork\n• Samples of student artwork\n• If contacted for an interview, please be prepared to share sample syllabi, professional work, and student work.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-japanese-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Japanese",
      "description": "Seton Hall University seeks applicants for an adjunct professor position in Japanese, beginning in Fall 2025. The position will be in the Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures within the College of Arts and Sciences. Candidates should have a Master’s degree or higher and experience teaching Japanese. The teaching load will be one to two courses per semester at the elementary, intermediate, or advanced levels.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, and they support its mission as a Catholic institution of higher education\nSalary: $3,600 for Master's/$3,900 for PhD per course taught, depending on credentials\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nProvide quality instruction and evaluation of students in various levels of Japanese courses. Create and administer assignments and tests, following Department guidelines. Maintain accurate student records in Canvas and follow all deadlines for timely submission of grades. Attend any faculty meetings pertinent to the instructor. Participate in Department events as needed. Specific duties depend on the course. Most courses meet twice a week with one to two office hours. Adjunct will also have the opportunity to provide a few paid hours of tutoring each week.\nRequired Qualifications:\n· Master's degree or higher\n· Fluency in Japanese\n· Experience teaching Japanese\n· Legal status to work in the U.S.\nDesired Qualifications:\n· Degree in Japanese preferred, but not required\n· Ability to incorporate technology into instruction and provide a well-rounded, interactive learning experience for students\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nFJ01 - Faculty Adjunct\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease send the following to Dr. Shigeru Osuka (shigeru.osuka@shu.edu), Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures, Seton Hall University:\n(1) Letter of application\n(2) Curriculum Vitae\n(3) List of at least two recommendations\nAll materials should be submitted electronically.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-professor-in-languages-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor in Languages",
      "description": "* This is a pool posting; applications will be held onto for two years.\nSeton Hall University seeks applicants for adjunct professor positions in Arabic, Chinese, Classics-Greek-Latin, French, German, italian, Japanese, Russian, and Spanish. The positions are in the Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures within the College of Arts and Sciences. Candidates should have a Master’s degree or higher and experience teaching languages. Teaching loads are one to two courses per semester at the elementary, intermediate, or Advanced levels. All classes are taught in person.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n· Provide high-quality, student-centered instruction across various levels.\n· Design, administer and evaluate student assignments and assessments in strict alignment with departmental curriculum guidelines.\n· Maintain regular communication with students and provide timely feedback on assessments.\n· Manage accurate student records in Canvas and follow all deadlines for timely submission of grades.\n· Coordinate periodically with full-time faculty and language program directors to ensure instructional alignment.\n· Participate in Department events as needed.\nSpecific duties and schedule depend on the course. Most courses meet twice a week with one to two office hours. Adjunct faculty also have the opportunity to provide some paid hours of tutoring each week.\nRequired Qualifications:\n· Master's degree or higher\n· Fluency in the language taught\n· Experience teaching languages\n· Legal status to work in the U.S.\nDesired Qualifications:\n· Degree in Applied Linguistics preferred, but not required\n· Experience with communicative and task-based teaching methodologies\n· Familiarity with ACTFL Guidelines\n· Ability to incorporate technology into instruction and provide a well-rounded, interactive learning experience for students\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1230 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1332.50 per credit for those with a terminal degree.\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nBenefits Information:\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/clinical-intern-supervisor-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Clinical Intern Supervisor",
      "description": "The Office of Fieldwork and Certification, within the College of Human Development, Culture and Media (CHDCM) at Seton Hall University, invites applications for Clinical Intern Supervisors. The Clinical Supervisor of pre-clinical and clinical interns is an important member of the clinical experience triad as an official representative of the university who directly supervises the teacher candidate. The clinical supervisor serves as a liaison between CHDCM and cooperating school personnel. The clinical supervisor aids in the education of teacher candidate by making sure that they are a thoughtful learner, leader, and community member. The Office seeks student-centered individuals who value excellence in teaching and commit to diversity, equity, inclusion, and collaboration.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Attend mandatory training sessions and orientations to meet with candidates prior to the first observation.\n• Work with the teacher candidate to design a supervision schedule and inform the teacher candidate and cooperating teacher of planned visits.\n• Complete lesson observations and evaluations, using designated online evaluation tools, according to the candidate’s clinical level.\n• Confer with the cooperating teacher relative to the teacher candidate's work and progress; conduct group conferences with the teacher candidate and cooperating teacher.\n• Communicate regularly with the candidate, cooperating teacher, and Office of Fieldwork and Certification.\n• Provide mentorship and feedback to the candidate to support their professional competency development.\n• Send early notification to the Office of Fieldwork and Certification of any challenges faced by the teacher candidate.\n• Provide specific suggestions and feedback about strategies for creating a positive classroom environment, developing classroom management skills, and acknowledging differences in the classroom to include all learners.\n• Complete tasks related to role as assigned course section instructor for supervisees, including verifying attendance and entering final grades.\nAbout the Department and Seton Hall University\nThe Office of Fieldwork and Certification serves all undergraduate and graduate programs within the College of Human Development, Culture, and Media. The office places candidates in their clinical experiences and culminating clinical practice. The placements reflect the college's commitment to preparing teachers and school personnel to serve all students. For more information, visit the Office website.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States, and its Carnegie classification is a doctoral university with high research activity. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Master's degree or acquired equivalent job experience\n• Minimum of three years of teaching and/or administrative experience\n• Experience supervising, consulting or otherwise working in an elementary/secondary school in contact with classroom teachers within the previous two years\n• Demonstrated expertise in the field in which he or she is supervising\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Experience using Microsoft Office Suite, such as Outlook and Teams, and student success software such as Watermark\n• Demonstrated record of teaching excellence and mentorship experience\nSalary Range:\n• Clinical Experience – $150 per student\n• Clinical Practice 1 – $400 per student\n• Clinical Practice 2 – $925 per student\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nApplications should include the following documents:\n• Cover letter detailing applicable experience, interest, qualifications for the position, and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion\n• Curriculum vitae or resume\n• Two professional references with contact information including addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses\nThe Office reviews applications on a rolling basis.\nBenefits Information:\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its faculty respects Catholic beliefs and values, engages the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and supports the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-pool-arabic-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor POOL - Arabic",
      "description": "* This is a pool posting; applications will be held onto for two years.\nSeton Hall University seeks applicants for an adjunct professor position in Arabic. The position is in the Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures within the College of Arts and Sciences. Candidates should have a Master’s degree or higher and experience teaching Arabic. Teaching loads are one to two courses per semester at the elementary, intermediate, or advanced levels.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, and they support its mission as a Catholic institution of higher education\nSalary: $3,600 for Master's/$3,900 for PhD per course taught, depending on credentials\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n· Provide quality instruction and evaluation of students in various levels of Arabic courses.\n· Create and administer assignments and tests, following Department guidelines.\n· Maintain accurate student records in Canvas and follow all deadlines for timely submission of grades.\n· Attend any meetings pertinent to the instructor.\n· Participate in Department events as needed.\nSpecific duties depend on the course. Most courses meet twice a week with one to two office hours. Adjuncts also have the opportunity to provide a few paid hours of tutoring each week.\nRequired Qualifications:\n· Master's degree or higher\n· Fluency in Arabic\n· Experience teaching Arabic\n· Legal status to work in the U.S.\nDesired Qualifications:\n· Degree in Arabic preferred, but not required\n· Ability to incorporate technology into instruction and provide a well-rounded, interactive learning experience for students\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-advertising-design-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Advertising & Design",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media of Seton Hall University invites applications for part-time adjunct instructors to teach in our on-campus Art and Design program. The College seeks to engage individuals who value creativity, diversity, inclusion, and collaboration.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach up to two courses per semester\n• Each class meets once a week for 2.5 hours\n• For additional information about the program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-art-design.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Expertise and ability to teach in one or more of the following areas: graphic design, advertising, UX/UI design, and digital photography\nLicenses and Certificates:\n• Earned graduate degree in visual art or design\n• A minimum of two years teaching experience at the college level\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral studio classroom environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nRequired Documents\n• Resume or Curriculum Vitae\n• Cover Letter\n• Three professional references with contact information: address, phone number, and email address\nOptional Documents\n• Samples of current artwork\n• Samples of student artwork\n• If contacted for an interview, please be prepared to share sample syllabi, professional work, and student work.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-higher-education-leadership-programs-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor – Higher Education Leadership Programs",
      "description": "The Department of Education Leadership, Management and Policy, within the College of Human Development, Culture and Media at Seton Hall University, invites applications for adjunct instructors to teach courses in higher education graduate programs. The Department seeks student-centered individuals who value excellence in teaching and commit to diversity, equity, inclusion, and collaboration. For an overview of higher education programs and courses, visit the Department website.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach one to two courses per semester\n• Teach in-person and hybrid modalities. Examples of class schedules include meetings once per week during weekdays, from 5:00 p.m. - 7:10 p.m. or 7:15 p.m. - 9:25 p.m., or four meetings across two weekends, from 9:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m., during the fall and spring semesters.\nThe Department of Educational Leadership, Management and Policy is dedicated to serving and developing aspiring and current leaders. Graduates pursue leadership roles in a variety of organizations, such as public and private P-12 schools, higher education institutions, law enforcement agencies, and non-profit and policy organizations. The Department offers a robust portfolio of certificate, master’s, educational specialist, and doctoral programs. Each program is individualized to meet students’ strengths and professional goals. The higher education unit, in particular, includes master’s and doctorate degrees. For more information, visit the Department website.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States, and its Carnegie classification is a doctoral university with high research activity. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Expertise and ability to teach in areas related to higher education. Areas include, but are not limited to, law; finance; leadership; student affairs; intercollegiate athletics; organization and governance; history of higher education; and qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methodologies\n• Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion\n• Earned doctorate in higher education or related field\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Minimum of one year teaching at the college level\n• Demonstrated record of teaching excellence\n• Experience with learning management systems such as Blackboard and Canvas\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nApplications should include the following documents:\n• Cover letter detailing applicable courses to teach, qualifications for the position and courses, and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion\n• Curriculum vitae or resume\n• Two professional references with contact information including addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses\nIf contacted for an interview, the Department will request, if appropriate, evidence of successful teaching, e.g., sample syllabi, student course evaluations, and anonymous student work with feedback.\nThe Department reviews applications on a rolling basis.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-animation-game-design-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Animation & Game Design",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media of Seton Hall University invites applications for part-time adjunct instructors to teach in our on-campus Art and Design program. The College seeks to engage individuals who value creativity, diversity, inclusion, and collaboration.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach up to two courses per semester\n• Each class meets once a week for 2.5 hours\n• For additional information about the program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-art-design.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Expertise and ability to teach in one or more of the following areas: animation, 3D computer graphics, and game design\n• Earned graduate degree in visual art or design\nLicenses and Certificates:\n• A minimum of two years teaching experience at the college level\n• Earned terminal degree in visual art or design\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral studio classroom environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nRequired Documents\n• Resume or Curriculum Vitae\n• Cover Letter\n• Three professional references with contact information: address, phone number, and email address\nOptional Documents\n• Samples of current artwork\n• Samples of student artwork\n• If contacted for an interview, please be prepared to share sample syllabi, professional work, and student work.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-professor-information-technology-management-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor - Information Technology Management",
      "description": "Department seeks IT professional to teach the required undergraduate introductory course in Information Technology.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach up to two sections of the required introductory course in IT. Each section will meet either twice per week for 75 minutes or once per week for 2.5 hours.\nRequired Qualifications:\nMaster's degree or higher in an IT related field or an MBA with work experience in IT.\nDesired Qualifications:\nPrior experience teaching courses in IT.\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-of-law-and-director-of-legal-externships-and-public-interest-newark-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Law and Director of Legal Externships and Public Interest",
      "description": "Seton Hall Law School seeks to hire an Assistant Professor of Law and Director of Legal Externships and Public Interest. This is a non-tenure track position. The Director works collaboratively with the Office of Career Services and the Associate Dean for Experiential Education, while maintaining primary responsibility for the academic integrity, assessment, and accreditation of the Externship Program. Core responsibilities include providing academic leadership of the externship curriculum, ensuring compliance with ABA standards, student advising and mentoring, placement development and employer outreach, and developing of public interest programming.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nExternship Program\n• Direct and administer the externship program to provide students with experiential learning opportunities that foster professional identity formation, ethical judgment, and the development of professional competencies.\n• Manage enrollment processes, eligibility determinations, registration procedures, and program communications for all externship offerings. Update materials and relevant websites as needed.\n• Update Memorandum of Understanding with externship placements and other contracts as needed.\n• Serve as the Law School’s primary administrator responsible for ensuring compliance with ABA Standard 304 and other applicable accreditation requirements governing field placement programs.\n• Develop and maintain relationships with judges, government agencies, public interest organizations, prosecutors' offices, public defenders, corporate legal departments, and law firms to expand and diversify externship opportunities for students. Recruit, evaluate, and approve new placements.\n• Monitor placement performance and implement corrective action to meet educational standards.\n• Develop instructional materials and teach the classroom component of the externship program, including seminars that integrate doctrine, professional identity formation, ethics, reflective practice, and professional skills development.\n• Communicate regularly with students regarding placement experiences.\n• Review supervisor evaluations of students and evaluate all assigned student work.\n• Maintain records to ensure compliance with ABA standards.\n• Develop, assess, and periodically revise student learning outcomes for the Externship Program; collect and analyze assessment data; and implement program improvements based on assessment results.\n• Prepare reports and recommendations regarding program effectiveness and student outcomes.\nPublic Interest Programming\n• Develop and implement strategic initiatives that advance the Law School's public interest mission and increase student engagement in public service careers in collaboration with Career Services and faculty.\n• Coordinate public interest programming, networking events, speaker programs, and related initiatives and develop relationships with public interest organizations and governmental agencies to expand student opportunities.\n• Advise students regarding public interest career opportunities, fellowships, and postgraduate opportunities.\n• Assist in administration of public interest funding, fellowships, loan repayment programs, stipends, or pro bono initiatives, as assigned.\n• Support fundraising for public interest initiatives and events.\nFaculty Teaching and Service\n• Participate in faculty meetings, committees, and other governance activities consistent with faculty status and university policies.\n• Engage in professional development and maintain familiarity with developments in experiential legal education and ABA accreditation requirements.\n• Represent Seton Hall Law at relevant events as appropriate.\n• Teach elective courses related to professional development and experiential learning as required and appropriate to meet faculty course load.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• J.D. degree from an ABA-accredited law school.\n• Admission to practice law in at least one U.S. jurisdiction.\n• Minimum of five years of legal practice or closely related professional experience, including experience in judicial, governmental, public interest, nonprofit, corporate, or private practice settings.\n• Demonstrated commitment to experiential legal education and student mentoring.\n• Excellent organizational, interpersonal, and written communication skills.\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Prior teaching experience in legal education.\n• Experience teaching or supervising students in experiential learning environments.\n• Experience supervising law students, attorneys, or legal professionals.\n• Experience administering externship, clinical, public interest, or experiential learning programs.\n• Familiarity with ABA accreditation standards governing field placements and experiential education.\n• Demonstrated experience developing professional relationships with legal employers, judges, government agencies, nonprofit organizations, or other legal placement partners.\nSalary Range:\n$92,965 - $103,295\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease submit three professional references.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage in servant leadership, and support the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-communication-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Communication",
      "description": "The College of  Human Development, Culture, and Media at Seton Hall University is seeking adjunct instructors for undergraduate, on campus, in person, online and hybrid delivered communication courses in the Communication program. Courses of particular interest are Communication Ethics, Persuasive Speaking, Public Presentational Speaking, and Organizational Communication.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach up to two sections of communication courses, as assigned. Course schedules vary with sections meeting either once or twice per week, online or in a hybrid format.\nFor additional information about the B.A. in Communication program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-communication.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\nMaster’s degree in Communication\nRelevant experience in instructional settings and delivery systems\nDesired Qualifications:\nPhD in Communication\nTwo years of teaching experience in higher education\nExperience teaching in online and hybrid environments\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral office environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease include electronic submission of your cover letter, resume/curriculum vitae, summary of teaching evaluations, sample syllabi, and unofficial transcripts for postsecondary coursework.\nQuestions about the position may be directed via email to the department chair, Dr. Renee Robinson, at renee.robinson@shu.edu.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-professor-of-business-writing-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor of Business Writing",
      "description": "Seton Hall University seeks adjunct instructors to teach sections of our Business Writing classes.\nThis course is designed to familiarize students with the tools and techniques necessary to become effective business communicators, with a special emphasis on business writing competencies and critical thinking. Professors of this course will instruct how to compose workplace writings such as emails, memos, business letters, reports, proposals, resumes, presentations, and data analysis. Key learning objectives in this course are audience awareness, analysis, grammar, syntax, format, organization, and presentation. At the end of the course, students will be familiar with the business library resources and the career center and will have an understanding and an ability to communicate effectively in the world of business.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, and they support its mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nCreating lesson plans; teaching 1-2 classes a week; holding office hours each week for students; grading and reviewing student work and exams; attending orientation and corresponding with the business writing coordinator, Prof. Gregory Iannarella.\nRequired Qualifications:\nCandidates must have experience teaching composition in a college or university setting and, at minimum, an MA in a related field. CV and letter of interest are required to apply; a statement of teaching philosophy, course evaluations from a composition class, and up to three letters of reference are recommended.\nDesired Qualifications:\nExperience in the business world is preferred; MBA/PhD preferred.\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/faculty-adjunct-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Faculty, Adjunct",
      "description": "The University CORE Curriculum at Seton Hall is seeking adjuncts (part time, nontenure-track) for the Academic Year of 2023-2024 to teach one or two sections of our Signature Core I and Core II courses. The courses are Journey of Transformation (Core I) and Christianity and Culture in Dialogue (Core II), and they are required for all Freshmen and Sophomores students. The courses consider the relationship between the Catholic intellectual tradition and other religions and cultures, particularly in terms of how they answer the big questions of life common to us all. Texts from the Christian tradition are studied along with texts from non-Christian traditions to demonstrate direct connections across other religions and cultures.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nThese courses are taught with an approach based on principles of dialogue with an eye to fostering a sense of community. The students are required to submit writing, both formal and informal, writing and to think critically.\nRequired Qualifications:\nMaster of Arts in Theology and/or Master of Divinity or other relevant area.\nDesired Qualifications:\nPh./D in humanities, social sciences, philosophy, or theology or related field preferred.\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-professional-psychology-family-therapy-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Professional Psychology & Family Therapy",
      "description": "The Department of Professional Psychology and Family Therapy is dedicated to serving the needs of aspiring mental health professionals in counseling, school counseling, school psychology, and counseling psychology. Graduates go on for their licensure/certification and become practicing counselors psychologists and marriage and family therapists, as well as receiving certificates in Sports Psychology, Drug and Alcohol Rehabilitation, and Applied Behavior Analysis. The Department offers a robust portfolio of certificate, master’s, educational specialists, and doctoral programs. Each program is individualized to meet the students’ strengths and professional goals. The doctoral program in Counseling Psychology is APA accredited and offers the student a 5-year program leading to the degree of Doctorate of Philosophy.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nInterested applicants with a minimum of a master’s degree in the mental health field may apply for an adjunct instructor position by completing the online application and submitting required supplemental materials. Candidates identified will be contacted for a required interview.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States, and its Carnegie classification is a doctoral university with high research activity. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives\nRequired Qualifications:\nMinimum of a master’s degree in the mental health field\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nInquiries regarding adjunct instructor opportunities can be directed to Dr. Thomas Massarelli, Department Chairperson, Professional Psychology and Family Therapy Department, via email to thomas.massarelli@shu.edu.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-theatre-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Theatre",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media of Seton Hall University is accepting applications for adjunct faculty to instruct in Acting and Vocal Techniques courses in the Theatre Program of the Department of Communication and the Arts, on the South Orange campus.\nOne of the country's leading Catholic universities, Seton Hall University has been developing students in mind, heart and spirit since 1856. Home to over 10,000 undergraduate and graduate students and offering more than 90 rigorous majors, Seton Hall prepares students to be leaders in their professional and community lives in a global society through their work with outstanding faculty in an evolving technologically advanced setting with values-centered curricula.\nSeton Hall's 58-acre\nmain campus\nis located in the quaint town of South Orange, New Jersey, only 14 miles from Manhattan accessible by Midtown Direct NJ Transit.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nAdjunct instructors may be assigned up to 6 credits per semester and are expected to comply with all university policies and procedures.\nRequired Qualifications:\nMFA\nAt least two years teaching experience at the college level\nExpertise in Acting and Vocal Techniques including Linklater and Miller certifications.\nSalary Range:\nminimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral office and classroom environments\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease include electronic submission of your cover letter, resume/curriculum vitae, summary of teaching evaluations, sample syllabi, and unofficial transcripts for postsecondary coursework.\nQuestions about the position may be directed via email to Prof. Pete Reader, at\npeter.reader@shu.edu\n.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-film-media-studies-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Film/Media Studies",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media at Seton Hall University is seeking adjunct instructors for undergraduate, on campus, in person film/media studies courses in the Visual and Sound Media Program. Courses of particular interest are Contemporary Cinema, Film Directors, Documentary Film, and Film Criticism.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach up to two sections of the Film or Media Studies courses, as assigned. Course schedules vary with sections meeting either once or twice per week.\nFor additional information about the B.A. in Visual and Sound Media program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-broadcast-visual-media.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\nMaster’s degree in Film or Media Studies\nRelevant experience in instructional settings\nDesired Qualifications:\nPhD in Film or Media Studies\n*Two years of teaching experience in higher education\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral office environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease include electronic submission of your cover letter, resume/curriculum vitae, summary of teaching evaluations, sample syllabi, and unofficial transcripts for postsecondary coursework.\nQuestions about the position may be directed via email to Dr. Jonathan Kraszewski, at jonathan.kraszewski@shu.edu.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/contract-faculty-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states-c56df0b6-33ba-41de-8b4e-e740a216fc22": {
      "title": "Contract Faculty",
      "description": "Seton Hall University’s Department of Mathematics and Computer Science invites applications for a contract faculty position in Cybersecurity to commence no later than August 16, 2026. The position includes undergraduate and graduate teaching responsibilities in Cybersecurity and related fields.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nCandidates must be qualified to teach courses in Cybersecurity and in areas of Computer Science related to Cybersecurity. We are particularly interested in candidates who can contribute to our M.S. program in Cybersecurity and provide hands-on lab experience for our students.\nIn addition to 12 credits of teaching per semester, this faculty member will have responsibilities for participating in recruitment and extracurricular activities and events for cybersecurity and related fields, including computer science and data science and engineering.\nRequired Qualifications:\nApplicants should have a masters degree in Cybersecurity, Computer Science, or a closely related field in hand by the time of appointment. While a Ph.D. is preferred, outstanding candidates with substantial, recent, and relevant industry experience as well as a masters and teaching experience will receive full, serious consideration. Successful applicants will demonstrate the potential to teach effectively at both the undergraduate and graduate levels.  Additionally, all applicants should demonstrate substantial potential for supervising student projects in Cybersecurity and knowledge of emerging trends in cybersecurity education, including hands-on labs and simulations.\nDesired Qualifications:\nPrior college or university teaching experience in Cybersecurity or related Computer Science fields.\nIndustry experience.\nSalary Range:\n$72,465-$80,517\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nProvide a cover letter, resume/CV, statement of teaching philosophy, research statement, diversity statement, and three letters of reference.\nBenefits Information:\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its faculty respects Catholic beliefs and values, engages the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and supports the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-of-cybersecurity-and-policy-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Cybersecurity and Policy",
      "description": "Seton Hall University’s Department of Mathematics and Computer Science and the School of Diplomacy and International Relations invite applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position in cybersecurity and policy to commence in the 2026-2027 academic year. The position will be a joint appointment housed in the College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, and with undergraduate and graduate teaching responsibilities each year for Computer Science (3 courses/9 credits) and Diplomacy and International Relations (3 courses/9 credits).\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nCandidates must be qualified to teach courses in the fields of 1) computer science related to cybersecurity, 2) government policy related to cyber security, information security, and cyber warfare, and 3) modern statistical research methods/data analysis.  We are particularly interested in candidates who can contribute to our degree programs in cybersecurity and international and homeland security and help launch our new curriculum in security and conflict analysis.\nRequired Qualifications:\nApplicants should have a Ph.D. in hand by the time of their appointment, either in 1) computer science, computer engineering, or cybersecurity, accompanied by academic training in public policy or extensive policy experience; or 2) political science or a related discipline accompanied by academic computer science training or extensive technical experience.  Successful applicants will demonstrate the potential to teach effectively at both the undergraduate and graduate levels.  Additionally, all applicants should have strong research backgrounds or demonstrate substantial potential for conducting important academic research in their field of specialization.\nSalary Range:\n$98,625-$109,583\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nProvide a cover letter, resume/CV, statement of teaching philosophy, research statement, diversity statement, and three letters of reference.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 3,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally inclusive community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an equal opportunity employer. It honors diverse perspectives and welcomes employees from all faith traditions. In turn, its faculty respects Catholic beliefs and values, engages the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and supports the University’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-professor-psychopharmacology-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor-Psychopharmacology",
      "description": "The Psychology Department at Seton Hall University seeks an adjunct instructor to teach an in-person course in Psychopharmacology starting January 2026. This 3000-level (i.e., upper division) course is required of students in our BS in Cognitive Neuroscience, and a potential elective for students in the Psychology major.\nCourse Description and select learning objectives from the Catalogue: “A comprehensive examination of the mechanisms by which drugs interact with the brain and behavior, with emphasis on the role of neurotransmitter systems.\n• Describe the field of psychopharmacology and its relation to other subfields within psychology and neuroscience.\n• Explain how pharmacokinetic factors interact to determine drug bioavailability.\n• Identify the main classes of abused drugs, describe their mechanisms of action and their behavioral effects\n• Identify the major classes of drugs used in the treatment of major psychiatric and neurological disorders, describe their mechanisms of action and their behavioral effects “\nIn the Psychology Department, all new adjunct faculty are paired with full-time faculty mentors to help the individual develop their teaching through conversation and course observation. Additionally, all adjunct faculty at Seton Hall enjoy free access to on-campus fitness facilities.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and support the university’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach one section of an in-person course in Psychopharmacology. This course will be scheduled in a once-a-week, 2.5-hour evening time slot on either Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday or Thursday. The course will be capped at 25-30 students and run from January 14, 2026, to the end of finals week.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA master's degree in psychology, neuroscience, or a closely related field plus relevant graduate coursework in psychopharmacology.\nDesired Qualifications:\n- Ph.D. in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a closely related field strongly preferred\n- Prior University teaching experience\nLicenses and Certificates:\nNone\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nApplicant should suggest at least two references whom we can contact\nBenefits Information:\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-computer-science-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor-Computer Science",
      "description": "The Department of Mathematics and Computer Science at Seton Hall University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position at the rank of assistant professor in Computer Science to start in August 2026.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, engage the Catholic Intellectual Tradition, and support the university’s mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nPriority will be given to applicants with research in areas of Computer Engineering, Robotics, or Cybersecurity. Depending on the applicant’s academic background, teaching responsibilities will include courses in Computer Science at all undergraduate levels and/or undergraduate/graduate courses in Cybersecurity or Data Science, including service courses. In addition, supervision of student research and maintaining a computer engineering lab is expected.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA PhD in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, or related area is required. The successful applicant will have a strong interest in teaching, in research, and in providing student research experiences. The candidate’s Ph.D. must be completed by the start of employment.\nDesired Qualifications:\nTeaching experience, a publication record, and successful grant funding are desirable\nSalary Range:\n$98,500-$109,500\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nAll applicants are required to provide a teaching statement, research statement and diversity statement (explaining what diversity means to the applicant with respect to Computer Science and to a university community).\nPlease also include a statement waiving the right to see reference letters.\nBenefits Information:\nFull Time Benefits\nLife today is complex. That’s why Seton Hall University offers a comprehensive package of benefits and programs to help you simplify and enrich your life.\nThese benefits are important elements of your total university compensation package. Benefits include but are not limited to bundled medical, prescription, and vision insurance, dental insurance, and life insurance, and retirement plan. More information is available at\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\n.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-professor-writing-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor - Writing",
      "description": "Seton Hall University seeks adjunct instructors to teach sections of our first-year writing classes. These are composition courses with an emphasis on rhetorical analysis. They fall into two categories: English 1201 is a composition course with a rhetorical focus, stressing textual and visual analysis, while English 1202 is an academic writing course focused on literature. Candidates must have experience teaching composition in a college or university setting and, at minimum, an MA in a related field. CV and letter of interest are required to apply; a statement of teaching philosophy, course evaluations from a composition class, and up to three letters of reference are recommended.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States and is home to about 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students. A vibrant and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives. Seton Hall University is an Equal Opportunity employer. It honors diversity and respects the religious commitments of all its employees. In turn, its employees respect Catholic beliefs and values, and they support its mission as a Catholic institution of higher education.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach 1-2 classes a semester. Classes meet 2-3 times a week. Instructors also hold office hours and grade/review assignments, as well as attend orientation and meetings with first-year writing director.\nRequired Qualifications:\nM.A. in related field and experience teaching composition in a college or university setting.\nDesired Qualifications:\nPh.D. in related field.\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nCV and letter of interest are required to apply; a statement of teaching philosophy, course evaluations from a composition class, and up to three letters of reference are recommended.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-professor-introduction-to-ethics-and-introduction-to-biomedical-ethics-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor-Introduction to Ethics and Introduction to Biomedical Ethics",
      "description": "Adjunct professors for Introduction to Ethics and Introduction to Biomedical Ethics.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeaching the course and grading student work.\nRequired Qualifications:\nMA in Philosophy minimum, Ph.D. preferred.\nDesired Qualifications:\nPh.D. in Philosophy or closely related field\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-k-12-education-leadership-programs-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - K-12 Education Leadership Programs",
      "description": "The Department of Education Leadership, Management and Policy, within the College of Human Development, Culture and Media at Seton Hall University, invites applications for adjunct instructors to teach courses in K-12 education leadership graduate programs. The Department seeks student-centered individuals who value excellence in teaching and commit to diversity, equity, inclusion, and collaboration. For an overview of K-12 leadership programs and courses, visit the Department website.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach one to two courses per semester\n• Teach hybrid and online modalities. Hybrid courses include a mixture of in-person and synchronous and asynchronous online learning. In-person meetings occur on four days across two weekends, from 9:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m., during the fall, spring, and summer semesters. Online courses include synchronous and asynchronous learning throughout the semester.\nThe Department of Educational Leadership, Management and Policy is dedicated to serving and developing aspiring and current leaders. Graduates pursue leadership roles in a variety of organizations, such as public and private P-12 schools, higher education institutions, law enforcement agencies, and non-profit and policy organizations. The Department offers a robust portfolio of certificate, master’s, educational specialist, and doctoral programs. Each program is individualized to meet students’ strengths and professional goals. The K-12 education leadership unit, in particular, includes certificate courses and master’s, education specialist, and doctorate degrees. For more information, visit the Department website.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States, and its Carnegie classification is a doctoral university with high research activity. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Expertise and ability to teach in areas related to K-12 education leadership. Areas include, but are not limited to, law; policy; finance; technology; curriculum; leadership; organizational theory; and, qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methodologies\n• Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion\n• Earned doctorate in K-12 education leadership or related field\nDesired Qualifications:\n• Minimum of one year teaching at the college level\n• Demonstrated record of teaching excellence\n• Demonstrated record of leadership experience\n• Experience with learning management systems such as Blackboard and Canvas\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nApplications should include the following documents:\n• Cover letter detailing applicable courses to teach, qualifications for the position and courses, and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion\n• Curriculum vitae or resume\n• Two professional references with contact information including addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses\nIf contacted for an interview, the Department will request, if appropriate, evidence of successful teaching, e.g., sample syllabi, student course evaluations, and anonymous student work with feedback.\nThe Department reviews applications on a rolling basis.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-art-history-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Art History",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media of Seton Hall University invites applications for part-time adjunct instructors to teach on-campus in our Art History program. The College seeks to engage individuals who value creativity, diversity, inclusion, and collaboration.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach up to two courses per semester\n• Each class meets twice a week for a combined 2.5 hours\n• For additional information about the program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-art-history.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\n• Expertise and ability to teach in one or more of the following areas: Art of the Western World (Western art survey from prehistory to present)\n• Earned PhD degree in art history\nLicenses and Certificates:\n• A minimum of one year teaching experience at the college level\n• Earned terminal degree in art history\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nRequired Documents\n• Resume or Curriculum Vitae\n• Cover Letter\n• Three professional references with contact information: address, phone number, and email address\nOptional Documents\n• Samples of current writing\n• Samples of student writing\n• If contacted for an interview, please be prepared to share sample syllabi, writing, and student work, as applicable.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-master-of-arts-in-museum-professions-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Master of Arts in Museum Professions",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media of Seton Hall University invites applications for part-time adjunct instructors to teach in our on-campus MA in Museum Professions program. The College seeks to engage individuals who value creativity, diversity, inclusion, and collaboration.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\n• Teach up to two courses per semester\n• Each class meets once a week for 2.5 hours\n• For additional information about the program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ma-museum-professions.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\nExpertise and ability to teach in one or more of the following areas: archives management, audience research and evaluation, exhibition development, history and theory of museums, legal and ethical issues in museums, museum activism and social issues, museums and communities, museum education, museum fundraising and development, museum leadership and management, museum registration/collections management, or museum technologies.\nLicenses and Certificates:\n• Earned graduate degree in museum studies, museum education, or related field\n• Minimum five years professional experience working in, with, or for museums\n• A minimum of two years teaching experience at the college level (preferred)\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nRequired Documents\n• Resume or Curriculum Vitae\n• Cover Letter\n• Three professional references with contact information: address, phone number, and email address\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-journalism-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor,  Journalism",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media at Seton Hall University is seeking adjunct instructors for undergraduate, on campus, in person courses in the Journalism program. One course of particular interest is Writing for the Media, an introductory course focused on news writing, public relations writing, and reporting.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach up to two sections of courses, as assigned. Course schedules vary with sections meeting either once or twice per week.\nFor additional information about the B.A. in Journalism program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-journalism.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\nExperience teaching at the college level\nWork experience in journalism/media/communications\nA graduate degree that includes at least 18 credit-hours of relevant coursework\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral Office Environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease include electronic submission of your cover letter, resume/curriculum vitae, summary of teaching evaluations, sample syllabi, unofficial transcripts for postsecondary coursework, and three professional/academic references.\nQuestions about the position may be directed via email to Dr. Matthew Pressman, at matthew.pressman@shu.edu.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-music-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Music",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media at Seton Hall University is seeking adjunct instructors for undergraduate, on campus, in person courses in the Music program. Courses rooted in applied music and music theory and history are of particular interest.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nAdjunct instructors may be assigned up to 6 credits per semester and are expected to comply with all university policies and procedures.\nRequired Qualifications:\nD.M.A. or Ph.D. in Music (or equivalent professional experience)\nAt least two years teaching experience at the College level\nExpertise in either music theory, history, or applied music\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral office and classroom environments\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease include electronic submission of your cover letter, resume/curriculum vitae, summary of teaching evaluations, sample syllabi, and unofficial transcripts for postsecondary coursework.\nQuestions about the position may be directed via email to Dr. Dena Levine, at dena.levine@shu.edu.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-sports-media-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Sports Media",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media at Seton Hall University is seeking adjunct instructors for undergraduate, on campus, in-person sports media courses in the Visual and Sound Media Program. Courses of particular interest are Introduction to Sports Media.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach up to two sections of the Sports Media courses, as assigned. Course schedules vary with sections meeting either once or twice per week.\nFor additional information about the B.A. in Visual and Sound Media program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-broadcast-visual-media.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\nMaster’s degree in Sports Media\nRelevant experience in instructional settings\nExperience in the sports media industry\nDesired Qualifications:\nPhD in Sports Media or Media Studies\nTwo years of teaching experience in higher education\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral office environment\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease include electronic submission of your cover letter, resume/curriculum vitae, summary of teaching evaluations, sample syllabi, and unofficial transcripts for postsecondary coursework.\nQuestions about the position may be directed via email to Assistant Professor, Dr. Branden Buehler, at branden.buehler@shu.edu.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    },
    "https://jobs.shu.edu/jobs/adjunct-instructor-oral-communication-south-orange-new-jersey-united-states": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Oral Communication",
      "description": "The College of Human Development, Culture, and Media at Seton Hall University is seeking adjunct instructors for several daytime, on-campus (in person) sections of the undergraduate basic course, Oral Communication. This course blends basic Communication principles, Public Speaking, and Interpersonal/Group Communication.\nSeton Hall University is located in South Orange, New Jersey, just 14 miles from New York City. It is the oldest Catholic diocesan institution of higher education in the United States. It is home to approximately 6,300 undergraduate and 4,700 graduate students from all 50 states and more than 60 foreign countries. An exciting and culturally diverse community, Seton Hall pursues academic excellence and ethical development in a collaborative environment and prepares its students to be servant leaders in their professional and community lives.\nDuties and Responsibilities:\nTeach up to two sections of the Oral Communication course, as assigned. Course schedules vary with sections meeting either once or twice per week.\nFor additional information about the B.A. in Communication program, visit https://www.shu.edu/academics/ba-communication.cfm\nRequired Qualifications:\nA master's degree in communication or a closely related field\nRelevant experience in instructional settings\nDesired Qualifications:\nAvailable on Monday and Wednesday (mornings or early afternoons) and Tuesday and Thursday (mornings or early-midafternoons).\nLicenses and Certificates:\nA master's degree in communication or a closely related field\nRelevant experience in instructional settings\nSalary Range:\nMinimum pay is $1200 per credit for those without a terminal degree and $1300 per credit for those with a terminal degree.\nExempt/Nonexempt:\nExempt\nBenefits Information:\nFor details about employee benefits, see:\nhttps://www.shu.edu/human-resources/benefits.html\nSeton Hall University offers supplemental retirement benefits.\nPhysical Demands:\nGeneral office and classroom environments\nSpecial Instructions to Applicants:\nPlease include electronic submission of your cover letter and resume/curriculum vitae.\nQuestions about the position may be directed via email to the coordinator for the course: Dr. Evelyn Plummer at evelyn.plummer@shu.edu.\nSeton Hall University is committed to programs of Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO)."
    }
  },
